<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Applies to every `dotnet test` in this repository, wired in by Directory.Build.props.

  TestSessionTimeout is here because a deadlocked test used to hang the suite
  indefinitely rather than fail it: nothing in dotnet test, the CI job or the local
  gate bounds a test run, so a stuck reader waits for as long as anybody lets it.
  A run past this bound is not a slow machine, it is a hang, and a hang
  that fails is worth more than one nobody can interrupt.

  The bound is three times the whole solution suite on a machine with the game
  prepared, and the game-free CI job is a fraction of a second of that. It is
  re-measured when the suite grows past it rather than being left to fire on
  ordinary load: fifteen minutes was three times a suite measured at 4m57s, and by
  the time the suite reached 10m12s that same bound was 1.5x and aborted a passing
  run on a machine that happened to be building something else at the time. A bound
  that fires on contention stops meaning "this is a hang", which is the only thing
  it is for. Thirty minutes is three times 10m12s, measured across all four projects
  on 2026-09-06. Raise it against a fresh measurement rather than removing it.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>1800000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
